Subject: [PATCH 0006] product: declare the Adze-specific product.json keys
Refs: ADR-0010
Status: UNWRITTEN — header and intent only, no diff hunks. See below.

STATUS
======

This file contains no diff. It is disabled by its `.patch.no` suffix.

INTENT
======

Add the `adze` key to upstream's `IProductConfiguration` interface so that our
workbench contribution can read it through `IProductService` under
`strictNullChecks` without a cast.

The delta writes this object (apps/ide/branding/product.delta.jq):

  "adze": {
    "agentHostProtocolVersion": "1",
    "engineChannel": "in-process",
    "updateFeedKind": "static-json"
  }

Namespaced under one key on purpose: a single object cannot collide with any future
upstream key, whereas three top-level keys are three chances to collide, and a
collision would be resolved silently by whichever assignment ran last.

FILES
=====

  src/vs/base/common/product.ts

The type declaration only. Patch 0004 may extend the extensionsGallery member of
the same interface with an "is this gallery signed" flag; if both patches land,
they touch the same file and should be generated together or ordered so that 0006
runs first.

WHY A PATCH AND NOT A DECLARATION MERGE
=======================================

A separate .d.ts in our own directory using declaration merging would keep this out
of upstream's tree, which is the pattern the rest of the fork follows.

It does not work here. IProductConfiguration is exported from a module rather than
declared globally, and interface merging across module boundaries requires a
`declare module` augmentation whose specifier is a relative path from a file inside
upstream's own compilation — which is a patch to upstream's tree with extra steps
and a worse failure mode when the path moves.

Six added lines in one low-churn file is the cheaper answer. `product.ts` did not
appear in ADR-0010's churn table because it is not a hotspot; the frozen
`productService.ts` next to it measured zero commits in 5.8 months.

RISK
====

Low. Additive lines inside an interface body conflict only when upstream edits the
same region, and the failure mode of a stale patch here is a TypeScript error
naming the exact file and line — the most diagnosable failure in the series.

HOW TO WRITE IT
===============

  1. Fetch the checkout.
  2. Add to IProductConfiguration:

       readonly adze?: {
         readonly agentHostProtocolVersion: string;
         readonly engineChannel: 'in-process' | 'daemon';
         readonly updateFeedKind: 'static-json';
       };

     Optional, and `readonly` throughout, matching the surrounding style.
  3. Keep it in sync with the delta. The type and the generated document are two
     halves of one contract, and nothing checks that they agree — a mismatch is a
     runtime undefined, not a compile error, because the key is optional.
  4. git -C apps/ide/vscode diff adze/pristine -- src/vs/base/common/product.ts \
       > apps/ide/patches/0006-product-service-type-additions.patch

Point 3 is the one that will actually bite. Consider adding an assertion to
apps/ide/scripts/verify-product-delta.sh that the generated `adze` object has
exactly the keys this patch declares.
